Implementation of build:
```cpp
void RecJPQInvertedLists::build(const uint8_t* codes, size_t N, size_t M, size_t B) {
    FAISS_THROW_IF_NOT(M > 0 && B > 0 && B <= 256);
    size_t nlists = M * B;
    offsets.assign(nlists + 1, 0);
    // Pass 1: count
    for (size_t i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        const uint8_t* c = codes + i * M;
        for (size_t m = 0; m < M; m++) {
            FAISS_THROW_IF_NOT(c[m] < B);  // hmm, per-element validation in the hot loop... do it once up front
            offsets[m * B + c[m] + 1]++;
        }
    }
    // Prefix sum
    for (size_t i = 0; i < nlists; i++) offsets[i+1] += offsets[i];
    item_ids.resize(N);
    // Pass 2: fill (use a copy of offsets as cursors)
    std::vector<size_t> cursor(offsets.begin(), offsets.end()-1);  // or reuse
    for (size_t i = 0; i < N; i++) {
        const uint8_t* c = codes + i * M;
        for (size_t m = 0; m < M; m++) {
            item_ids[cursor[m * B + c[m]]++] = (idx_t)i;
        }
    }
    M = M; B = B;
}
```

Validation: checking c[m] < B per element inside the loop is N*M comparisons — not a problem (build is one-off). But a FAISS_THROW inside a tight loop… I'll validate in a pre-pass (fast, vectorizable). Actually, let me do the count pass with the check included; throw on the first invalid value. The count pass is already O(N·M), so an added comparison is ~free.

Note: item_ids stores internal positions (idx_t). With ascending fill order, each posting list is in ascending internal-position order.

Memory: offsets is (M*B+1) * 8 bytes — for M=8, B=256: 2049*8 = 16KB. Trivial.

