Interesting — the `AddSaCodes` test is failing, with id mismatches. This looks like a real bug. Let me analyze.

In `add_sa_codes`, we're adding codes from `data2` with ids `10000 + data2.ids[i]`. `data2` is created via `make_recjpq(M, B, ds, 50, 121)` — default ids are sequential 0..49. So the new ids are 10000..10049.

Wait, but `ids2[i] = 10000 + data2.ids[i]`, and `data2.ids[i] = i` (sequential). So ids2 = 10000..10049. OK.

The failure is: at rank 13, expected 10038 but got 10045. At rank 14, expected 10042 but got 8. Hmm, these look like the search results are wrong — i.e., the score ordering is incorrect.

Wait — this might be a real bug in `add_sa_codes`. Let me think about the CSR construction in `invlists.build(item_codes.data(), (size_t)ntotal + n, M, B)`.

In `add_sa_codes`:
```cpp
const size_t ntotal_old = (size_t)ntotal;
item_codes.insert(item_codes.end(), codes, codes + (size_t)n * M);
...
invlists.build(item_codes.data(), (size_t)ntotal + n, M, B);
ntotal += n;
```

At the time of the `invlists.build` call, `ntotal` is still the old value (100). So `(size_t)ntotal + n = 100 + 50 = 150`. OK, this is correct. And item_codes now has 150*M entries. So build over 150 items. Correct.

Hmm, so that seems fine. Let me rethink.

Wait — the actual bug is in the test's expected value calculation. Let me look at the "combined catalog" setup in the AddSaCodes test. It's building `RecJPQData both`:
```cpp
both.codes = data1.codes;
both.codebooks = data1.codebooks;
both.ids = data1.ids;
for (size_t i = 0; i < data2.N; i++) {
    both.codes.insert(...data2.codes...);
    both.ids.push_back(10000 + data2.ids[i]);
}
both.id_to_pos.reserve(300);
```

Let me read the rest of this test to see how `both.id_to_pos` is set up and whether the exhaustive baseline is correct. Let me read lines 724-780.

Actually, wait. Let me rethink. The id at rank 13: expected 10038, got 10045. Both are in the 10000+ range (i.e., both from data2). So the score of id 10045 (data2 position 45) is higher than that of 10038 (data2 position 38)? If the exhaustive baseline computed the scores correctly, this is strange.

Actually — wait. This might be a floating-point precision issue in the exhaustive baseline, or in the tie-breaking at the boundary. Let me look: the check first validates scores (line 220: score_eq D[r] and exp.top_scores[r]) and that D[r] matches exp.all_scores[pos]. The id mismatch at line 254 only occurs when "there are no ties at the K-th boundary" (n_at_boundary == boundary_in_topk).

Hmm, but if scores match at each rank AND each id's score matches exhaustive, but the IDs differ… that means two items have exactly the same score (a tie), and exhaustive picked one ordering while search picked a different one, but the "no ties" check got fooled.

No wait, the more likely explanation is: the `both` catalog in the test isn't constructed to match what `add_sa_codes` produced in the index. Let me carefully read the entire test.

